            {
              "id": "f94e721f-e8b4-4ffb-9fd1-f098d150b3c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fprintf`, you can use `fputs` or `fwrite` which do not interpret the format string. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"[ERROR]: Invalid buffer as buffer length exceeded\\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
