            {
              "id": "14ad6ad0-065c-419f-a138-ee2a858e5db8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities. \n\nIn this case, the function `std::strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded context. This function returns a pointer to a string that describes the error code passed in the argument errno. The string is actually a static buffer that is shared by all threads, so if another thread calls `strerror`, it could overwrite the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives whenever possible. In this case, you can use `strerror_r` or `strerror_s` which are thread-safe versions of `strerror`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `std::strerror(errno)` with `strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf)` or `strerror_s(buf, sizeof buf, errno)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif(strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This header file contains definitions for functions to manipulate C strings and arrays.\n- `<cerrno>`: This header file contains macro definitions for error codes used by the functions to indicate different types of errors.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4d1f0608-ceaf-4736-bbf7-3f41ce2bd2cb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. \n\nIn this specific case, the function `std::getenv(name)` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in an environment block. The problem is that this block can be modified by another process, leading to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv(name)` in your code. Instead, consider using more secure alternatives that do not have the same vulnerabilities. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv(name)`, you can use the `std::getenv_s` function, which is a more secure alternative. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nsize_t requiredSize;\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, name);\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable doesn't exist.\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nchar* value = (char*)malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\nif (!value)\n{\n    printf(\"Failed to allocate memory.\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, name);\nprintf(\"%s\\n\", value);\nfree(value);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<stdlib.h>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6472215f-c578-4d57-9a7a-de3df849e77d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. \n\nIn this case, the `ctime` function is considered insecure. The `ctime` function converts a time_t value into a string, but it does not check for buffer overflows. If an attacker can control the input to the `ctime` function, they can potentially cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking. In the case of `ctime`, a safer alternative is `ctime_r`, which is a reentrant version that takes an additional argument for the result.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `ctime` function with `ctime_r`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\nvoid print_time(time_t time) {\n    char buffer[26];\n    if (ctime_r(&time, buffer) == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        printf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<ctime>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "14ad6ad0-065c-419f-a138-ee2a858e5db8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities. \n\nIn this case, the function `std::strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded context. This function returns a pointer to a string that describes the error code passed in the argument errno. The string is actually a static buffer that is shared by all threads, so if another thread calls `strerror`, it could overwrite the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives whenever possible. In this case, you can use `strerror_r` or `strerror_s` which are thread-safe versions of `strerror`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `std::strerror(errno)` with `strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf)` or `strerror_s(buf, sizeof buf, errno)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif(strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof buf) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This header file contains definitions for functions to manipulate C strings and arrays.\n- `<cerrno>`: This header file contains macro definitions for error codes used by the functions to indicate different types of errors.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
