            {
              "id": "1ccd48c9-f402-474e-92e1-350d599ac30f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly validated.\n\nThe `sscanf` function is used to read formatted input from a string. The function reads characters from the string, interprets them according to a specified format, and stores the results in its arguments. If the input is not properly validated, an attacker can provide a specially crafted string that can lead to buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `sscanf` function or any other insecure C library functions. Instead, use secure alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. Also, always validate user input to ensure it is within the expected range and format.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sscanf` function with a secure alternative. For example, you can use `strtok` and `strtol` functions to parse the string:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nchar *token = strtok(message, \":\");\nif (token != NULL) {\n    pid = strtol(token, NULL, 16);\n    token = strtok(NULL, \"\\n\");\n    if (token != NULL) {\n        strncpy(task_name, token, sizeof(task_name) - 1);\n        task_name[sizeof(task_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for `strtok` and `strncpy` functions.\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ffbba086-8126-4066-b1f0-e128209a61be",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully. This is because `sscanf` does not perform bounds checking on the input, and can therefore write beyond the end of the buffer if the input is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `sscanf` and similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking, such as `strncpy` or `snprintf`. Additionally, always validate and sanitize input before processing it to ensure it does not exceed the size of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sscanf` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `strncpy` and `strtol` to achieve the same result:\n\n```cpp\nchar *endptr;\nevent = strtol(event_in, &endptr, 16);\nstrncpy(message, endptr, sizeof(message) - 1);\nmessage[sizeof(message) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code first converts the hexadecimal number at the start of `event_in` to an integer using `strtol`, then copies the rest of the string into `message` using `strncpy`, ensuring not to exceed the size of `message`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `#include <stdio.h>`\n- `#include <stdlib.h>`\n- `#include <string.h>`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "acb6e67a-4b80-433b-97ac-4f703aad5762",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will keep reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(schema_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `schema_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Alternatively, consider using safer string handling functions that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure schema_key is null-terminated\nschema_key[schema_key.size()] = '\\0';\nfeature_line.substr(strlen(schema_key)).c_str();\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(schema_key, schema_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and `std::string::substr`\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2a76cfe7-664e-4c0e-a263-1b60cc249a2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be insecure because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflows. For example, instead of `sprintf`, use `snprintf` which takes an additional parameter specifying the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 23 + 5, sizeof(str) - 28, \"%08x\", uuid->asic_0);\n```\n\nThis code will prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that the number of bytes written into `str` does not exceed its size.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "587b806b-0acf-453a-8052-c33743112138",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(version_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if `version_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent buffer overflows, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Ensure version_key is null-terminated\nversion_key[version_key.size() - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Use strnlen instead of strlen\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(version_key, version_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and its member functions.\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen` and `std::strnlen`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8f3a0afa-896a-4443-87bf-1b1e2fbb7be9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are considered insecure because they do not perform bounds checking and can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code, the `sprintf` function is used, which is one of these insecure functions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that the number of characters written into the buffer does not exceed its size.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 19 + 5, sizeof(str) - 24, \"%04x\", uuid->asic_4);\n```\n\nThis code ensures that the number of characters written into `str` does not exceed its size minus the offset (24 in this case).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdio` or `stdio.h` for the `snprintf` function\n- `cstddef` or `stddef.h` for the `sizeof` function\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "78cfeeb7-18eb-4ca3-833d-d7c772a21ea8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, application crashes, and other serious security issues. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which does not check the size of the destination buffer and can easily cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that are available in the C++ Standard Library. For example, instead of `sprintf`, you can use `snprintf` which checks the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf`:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 18 + 5, sizeof(str) - 23, \"-\");\n```\n\nThis code will prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that the number of characters written into `str` does not exceed its size.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "203dd0e3-c85b-49e2-abaa-8c92fac698e5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, leading to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which does not check the size of the destination buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer alternatives to `sprintf` that include buffer size as a parameter, such as `snprintf`. This function ensures that the destination buffer will not overflow by taking an additional size argument to limit the amount of data written.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(str + 16 + 5, sizeof(str) - 21, \"%02x\", uuid->clk_seq_low);\n```\n\nThis code will prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than `sizeof(str) - 21` characters are written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<stdio.h>` for `snprintf`\n- `<uuid/uuid.h>` for `uuid_t`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
