            {
              "id": "88413dc3-5cfb-41e1-892d-35531848c918",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflow. This can lead to various security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure. In C++, one such function is `sprintf()`, which does not check the size of the destination buffer and can write more data than it can hold, causing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include buffer size as a parameter, thus preventing buffer overflow. In C++, these functions are often found in the `<cstdio>` library and include `snprintf()`, which is a safer alternative to `sprintf()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf()` function with `snprintf()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming str is a character array of sufficient size\nsnprintf(str + 18, sizeof(str) - 18, \"-\");\n```\n\nThis code will prevent writing more data to `str` than it can hold, thus preventing a buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstdio>` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
