            {
              "id": "7acb1b92-214f-434e-919c-7d00a4e7b4e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" refers to the use of C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nThe `sscanf()` function is used to read formatted input from a string. The function reads characters from the string, interprets them according to a specified format, and then stores the results in its arguments. If the format string is not properly validated or if the function is used incorrectly, it can lead to buffer overflows or other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sscanf()`. In C++, you can use the `std::istringstream` class from the `<sstream>` library, which provides a safer and more flexible way to parse strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string bdf_str = \"some string\";\nunsigned int domain, bus, device, function;\n\nstd::istringstream iss(bdf_str);\nchar ch; // to read the ':' and '.' characters\n\nif (iss >> std::hex >> domain >> ch >> bus >> ch >> device >> ch >> function) {\n    // parsed successfully\n} else {\n    // parsing failed\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "52bec3a2-49ae-424b-9c6d-dde955aaaa27",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sprintf` that perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`. This function takes an additional size argument which specifies the maximum number of bytes to be used in the output buffer, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nchar proc_id[PROC_ID_SIZE];\nsnprintf(proc_id, PROC_ID_SIZE, \"%d\", processor->get_processor_index());\n```\n\nIn this code, `PROC_ID_SIZE` is a constant that should be defined to be the size of the `proc_id` buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` or `<stdio.h>` for the `snprintf` function\n- The definition of the `processor` object and its `get_processor_index` method, which are not standard library features and would be defined in your own code or in a third-party library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links were active and accessible at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "44375478-cb23-48e8-82e5-8f10f80c9d60",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to information disclosure or unauthorized code execution if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, consider using a method that does not involve environment variables to pass sensitive information. If environment variables must be used, ensure they are properly sanitized and validated before use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming the use of Boost library\n#include <boost/optional.hpp>\n\nboost::optional<std::string> get_env(const char* name) {\n    if(const char* env_p = std::getenv(name)) {\n        return env_p;\n    }\n    return boost::none;\n}\n\n// Usage\nauto path_power_root_override = get_env(\"RSMI_DEBUG_PP_ROOT_OVERRIDE\");\nif(path_power_root_override) {\n    env_vars_.path_power_root_override = *path_power_root_override;\n} else {\n    // Handle the case when the environment variable is not set\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- C++ Standard Library\n- Boost Library (optional.hpp)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1f9e0713-5c3b-4b9e-8554-c5b67e0e3da4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `getenv` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. If you must use `getenv`, ensure that the returned value is properly sanitized and validated before use. Also, consider using secure coding practices to minimize the risk of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you could use a configuration file to store the required settings, and then read this file at runtime. This would avoid the potential for an attacker to manipulate the environment variable.\n\n```cpp\nstd::ifstream config_file(\"config.txt\");\nstd::string hwmon_root_override;\nif (config_file.is_open())\n{\n    std::getline(config_file, hwmon_root_override);\n    env_vars_.path_HWMon_root_override = hwmon_root_override;\n}\nconfig_file.close();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `getenv`\n- `<fstream>` for `std::ifstream`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "768ec8b8-d876-4eef-a96d-5afe90435700",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have the same vulnerabilities. If you must use `getenv`, ensure that the returned value is properly validated and sanitized before use. Also, consider using secure coding practices to minimize the risk of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you could use a configuration file to store the required values, and then read these values using a safe function. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n\n```cpp\nstd::ifstream config_file(\"config.txt\");\nstd::string drm_root_override;\nif (config_file.is_open())\n{\n    std::getline(config_file, drm_root_override);\n    env_vars_.path_DRM_root_override = drm_root_override;\n}\nconfig_file.close();\n```\n\nIn this example, the `RSMI_DEBUG_DRM_ROOT_OVERRIDE` value is read from a configuration file instead of from an environment variable.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdlib` for the `getenv` function\n- `fstream` for the `ifstream` class\n- `string` for the `string` class\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Code Review Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-code-review-guide/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e40971b-40c2-42f5-b470-3aa580c3c0b5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. If you must use them, ensure that you are handling their results and inputs carefully to avoid security issues. In the case of `getenv`, consider the possibility that the environment variable may not exist and that the function will return a null pointer. Also, be aware that the returned string may be modified by subsequent calls to `getenv`, `setenv`, or `unsetenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nconst char* ev_str = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nif(ev_str != nullptr)\n{\n    // Use ev_str\n}\nelse\n{\n    // Handle the case where ENV_VAR does not exist in the environment\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4e40971b-40c2-42f5-b470-3aa580c3c0b5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. If you must use them, ensure that you are handling their results and inputs carefully to avoid security issues. In the case of `getenv`, consider the possibility that the environment variable may not exist and that the function will return a null pointer. Also, be aware that the returned string may be modified by subsequent calls to `getenv`, `setenv`, or `unsetenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nconst char* ev_str = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nif(ev_str != nullptr)\n{\n    // Use ev_str\n}\nelse\n{\n    // Handle the case where ENV_VAR does not exist in the environment\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
