              "id": "aacf53c0-1006-4947-aba8-f61f07843e5d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or potential execution of malicious code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(info.container_name) > 0`, there is a potential risk if `info.container_name` is not a null-terminated string. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strlen()` function with `strnlen()`. The second parameter to `strnlen()` should be the maximum size of `info.container_name`. \n\n```cpp\nstrnlen(info.container_name, sizeof(info.container_name)) > 0\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for the original code. The `strnlen()` function is also part of the C standard library, but it is not available in all environments. If `strnlen()` is not available, you may need to implement a similar function yourself.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5998767b-245f-427a-aaea-52f3ecd9515a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(container_type_name[i])`, the vulnerability arises if `container_type_name[i]` is not a null-terminated string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to read.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `container_type_name` is an array of strings, you can ensure null-termination as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure null-termination\ncontainer_type_name[i][sizeof(container_type_name[i]) - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Now it's safe to call strlen\nsize_t len = strlen(container_type_name[i]);\n```\n\nIf you have control over the creation of the strings, ensure they are null-terminated at creation time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3635ff83-463b-4943-b6dc-8892eea606e9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives. In this case, use `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)` instead of `strerror(errno)`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror(errno)` function with `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    // Use buf\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`: This library is required for the `strerror_r()` function.\n- `errno.h`: This library is required for the `errno` variable.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1eeacfb5-39b2-4070-89c2-78838a49baf7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer string handling functions that include buffer size as a parameter, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info.container_name, container_id.c_str(), sizeof(info.container_name) - 1);\ninfo.container_name[sizeof(info.container_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `container_id` to `info.container_name`, but it will not write more than `sizeof(info.container_name) - 1` bytes. The last byte is set to the null character to ensure that `info.container_name` is a null-terminated string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d82220fb-e54c-4084-933e-c80879f5a65b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`, or `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`. In this case, you could use `std::istringstream` or `std::stoi` to parse the string in a safer way.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string fd_bdf_str;\nstd::istringstream iss(bdfline);\nstd::string temp;\nif (iss >> temp >> fd_bdf_str) {\n    // Successfully parsed\n} else {\n    // Parsing failed\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0bcbc758-1139-441e-9941-dcd6f7fb6525",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `strerror()` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-reentrant functions like `strerror()`. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives like `strerror_r()` in POSIX or `strerror_s()` in Windows. Also, consider using exception handling for error reporting instead of relying on error codes and error strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) != 0) {\n    std::cerr << \"Failed to get error message for errno: \" << errno << std::endl;\n} else {\n    std::cerr << \"Failed to open /proc/cpuinfo: \" << buf << std::endl;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- iostream\n- cstring\n- cerrno\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "773a5d45-776a-4a61-a911-f3290233176e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backward compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is identified as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. This function seems to be a custom function, not a standard C or C++ function. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could lead to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the unsafe functions with safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other safety measures. If the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is using unsafe functions internally, you should revise its implementation to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using unsafe C functions, you should replace them with safer alternatives. For example, if it's using `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` and make sure to null-terminate the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "773a5d45-776a-4a61-a911-f3290233176e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backward compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is identified as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. This function seems to be a custom function, not a standard C or C++ function. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could lead to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the unsafe functions with safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other safety measures. If the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is using unsafe functions internally, you should revise its implementation to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using unsafe C functions, you should replace them with safer alternatives. For example, if it's using `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` and make sure to null-terminate the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
