            {
              "id": "77ac4097-588b-4ba7-bb83-f9b4720d882b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy()` instead of `strcpy()`, `snprintf()` instead of `sprintf()`, and so on. In this case, you can use `std::istringstream` or `std::stoi` instead of `sscanf()`. \n\nAlso, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent buffer overflow and other types of attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::istringstream iss(line);\nstd::string str;\nlong correctable_count;\n\nif (!(iss >> str >> correctable_count)) {\n    // handle error\n}\nerr_cnt->correctable_count = correctable_count;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>` for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
