              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `fgets()` instead of `gets()`, or `snprintf()` instead of `sprintf()`. In this case, you can use `std::istringstream` or `std::stoi` instead of `sscanf()`. \n\nAlso, always ensure that the input is properly validated and controlled. Avoid using functions that do not check the size of the input against the size of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sscanf()` function with a safer alternative. Here is a possible fix using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::istringstream iss(line);\nchar single_char;\nint sleep_freq;\nstd::string str;\n\nif (!(iss >> single_char >> sleep_freq >> str) || iss >> std::ws && iss.get() != EOF) {\n    // Handle error: input format was not as expected\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>` for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
